<section class="section" aria-labelledby="months-heading">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Month by Month</p>
    <h2 id="months-heading">What the habitat is doing</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Timings shift with each year's rain. Flowering and migration notes describe typical patterns for dry
      deciduous forest in south-eastern Rajasthan, not fixed dates.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table seasons-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Month</th><th scope="col">Weather</th><th scope="col">Habitat</th><th scope="col">Life to notice</th><th scope="col">Care point</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">January</th><td>Coldest month; cold nights, occasional frost and one or two winter showers.</td><td>Grasses dry and golden; reservoirs and river pools full from the monsoon.</td><td>Winter ducks, geese, waders and gulls at reservoirs and the Chambal; the Asian Waterbird Census season.</td><td>Keep distance from roosting and feeding flocks.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">February</th><td>Days warm up, nights still cool.</td><td>Leaf fall begins in many deciduous trees; ground layer opens up.</td><td>Palash (<em>Butea monosperma</em>) starts its orange flowering; winter birds still present.</td><td>Fire season starts (the TCP gives February–June).</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">March</th><td>Steady rise in temperature.</td><td>Most trees bare or thin; dry leaf litter builds up.</td><td>Mahua flowers; winter migrants begin to leave.</td><td>Dry litter makes fire spread fast; never drop matches or bidis.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">April</th><td>Hot and dry.</td><td>Seasonal nalas dry out; water shrinks to a few pools and reservoirs.</td><td>Animals concentrate near remaining water; summer migrants such as the Golden Oriole arrive.</td><td>Water points are sensitive: no public sharing of locations.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">May</th><td>Hottest month, up to about 46 °C; pre-monsoon showers possible.</td><td>The “pinch period”: little water, little fodder, high stress for wildlife.</td><td>Shade trees and rocky shelter matter most; some birds begin nesting.</td><td>Heat risk for people and animals; avoid disturbing anything near water.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">June</th><td>Hot first half; pre-monsoon showers in the second half drop temperatures by 3–5 °C.</td><td>First rains wet the soil; new leaves appear.</td><td>Indian Pitta and Pied Cuckoo arrive with the monsoon; frogs begin calling.</td><td>Breeding season for many species: keep away from nests and young.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">July</th><td>South-west monsoon.</td><td>Grasses and herbs shoot up; streams flow; ponds fill.</td><td>Peak insect and amphibian activity; pittas calling in the understorey.</td><td>Unpaved tracks become slippery; visitor routes may be closed for the monsoon, so check official notices.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">August</th><td>Heavy monsoon rain.</td><td>Full green canopy; tall grass; seasonal waterfalls in gorges.</td><td>Butterflies, dragonflies, monsoon flowering trees and fungi.</td><td>Rising water in nalas and rivers can be sudden.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">September</th><td>Monsoon withdraws, usually by mid-month.</td><td>Grasses flower and seed; wetlands at their fullest.</td><td>Water lilies on ponds; resident ducks; early passage migrants.</td><td>Grassland birds nest on the ground: stay on tracks.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">October</th><td>Hot days, cooler nights.</td><td>Grass begins to dry; the forest starts to open up again.</td><td>Wagtails, pipits, larks and the first winter migrants arrive.</td><td>Watch for early fires as grass dries.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">November</th><td>After mid-month, day and night temperatures fall.</td><td>Clear, open woodland; reservoirs still full.</td><td>Ducks and geese build up; raptors and harriers over grassland.</td><td>Wetland edges are crowded with birds: keep vehicles back.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">December</th><td>Cool and dry.</td><td>Golden grassland, thinning leaves; good visibility.</td><td>Peak winter waterbird numbers; Sarus Cranes in farmland and wetland edges.</td><td>Farm-edge wildlife depends on undisturbed fields.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="source-note">
    Climate, fire season and the pinch period: <a href="/resources/documents/mhtr-tiger-conservation-plan-bhainsrodgarh-core-addition/">Tiger Conservation Plan</a>
    (sections 2.1.6, 2.1.7, 2.5 and pinch-period management). Migrant status of Indian Pitta, Pied Cuckoo and Golden
    Oriole follows the <a href="/biodiversity/">biodiversity reference list</a>. Visitor access and closures are set by
    the Rajasthan Forest Department; always check the <a href="https://obms-tourist.rajasthan.gov.in/wildlife-attraction" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official OBMS portal</a>.
  </p>
</section>

